WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
Good morning John. I enjoyed your zombie list very much though it was woefully lacking in World War Z

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:08.800
which is the only piece of media that has given me nightmares since "Akira".

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.520
I don't know if you know this, but Dad took me to see "Akira"...when I was 10

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.140
...in a dinner theater...

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.880
...and I will never forgive him.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.740
(music plays) There's a song that plays during a particularly creepy part of the movie

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.740
and I'm playing it for you right now, though I'm not listening to it

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.020
because if I was listening to it I would look like this

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.680
(Heavy breathing)

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.060
In my opinion, that song is the creepiest piece of media that has ever been created.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.000
John, here's what I just did

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
I just took a copyrighted work...and then I commented on it.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.400
And that is what we call "Fair Use"

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.620
because you can't just steal copyrighted stuff and then

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.600
and then, like, monetize it and be like "It's mine!"

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.740
But you can can comment on a copyrighted work

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.680
and then that comment can include pieces of the copyrighted work.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.000
Making that illegal would be a violation of free speech

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.760
but Fair Use is still a little bit fuzzy under the law

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.260
so the people who created "Akira" could come in and challenge me on that.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.260
So I do think that American copyright law needs to be better.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.160
I love copyright law! We need copyright law!

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.960
John, I don't want people to steal your books.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.080
But I also do not want Congress

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.400
sticking it's smarmy, dirty little fingers into my internet.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.100
I'm sorry Congress, I know you guys get a bad rap...

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.080
...but don't mess with my internet or I WILL COME AT YOU!

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.040
So John, I have a top 5 list for you today.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.660
It's my "Top 5 Reasons Why we Should Not Let Congress Regulate the Internet"

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.620
One: I don't want to go to jail!

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.120
Also, I don't want 95 percent of people under 30 to be in jail!

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.560
I just think it would be really BAD for the American economy if we were all in jail.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.620
Number two: one of the current pieces of legislation is called:

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:36.120
The Preventing Real Online Threats to Economic Creativity and Theft of Intellectual Property Act

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.060
...and I feel like I should be doing this, like a politician

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.260
I don't know why they think that this is the hand motion they should always be using.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.680
And the name of that act makes me want to co-sponser a bill called

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:52.960
the Anti Contrived Representations of Name Yielding Mnemonics Seriously Shut Up Congress Act

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.800
Three: Anti-piracy legislation stifles creativity

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.820
and it stifles economic growth.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.000
Machinima is a great example of a very large company doing very well in this economy

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.360
that basically does things that are illegal but no one cares that they're illegal because they're actually good for the copyright holder.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.000
If copyright holders are happy about copyright infringement,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.960
then our copyright legislation is clearly not too lax.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.140
Four: we cannot blame a site for content that its users upload.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.320
Think about Tumblr, which is made wonderful by all the

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.680
GIFs of Abed saying whatever Abed said last night on Community.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.380
The PROTECT IP Act would make Tumblr responsible for keeping all of those off the site

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:31.420
and if it was found that they weren't doing everything they could to keep those GIFs off the site

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.440
they could be turned off.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.480
Like a flip of the switch. No more Tumblr.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.720
And finally number 5: we really should not be putting the power into the hands of the corporations here.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.740
Under the PROTECT IP Act, a corporation could flag a VlogBrothers video for being

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.540
in violation of its copyright. The video would then

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.180
be taken down, without any review

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.220
by a person to figure out whether or not it has copyright infringement.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.800
We, the little content creators that we are,

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.320
could then do NOTHING to get it back online

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.860
Because apparently, the Motion Picture Association of America is

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.860
is now a law enforcement body.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.320
The passive explosion of good, creative content

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.540
and art and ideas that has come out of the internet

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.380
is just, it's a good thing

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.160
and we should not be throwing a wrench into this process!

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.920
And yes, technology is forcing this change; we are being forced to change

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.180
but we are going to have to change

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:18.040
Trying to lock it down by screwing up the only really good economic engine that we have

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:18.540
right now

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.780
is..is just...GUUUAHHHH!

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:22.860
Everyone, check down to the description below, where I have

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.420
written some stuff about how you can help keep the internet free

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.720
John, I will see you on..rrgh, I don't know which day...

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.180
...what day is it. What day is it?

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.900
God. I'll see you on Monday.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.860
(singing) We're are having a blizzzzzzard!

